WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.222
Selection sort can be described recursively

00:00:04.222 --> 00:00:05.649
in three steps.

00:00:05.649 --> 00:00:09.660
Scan all n elements to find the largest one.

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:12.085
Swap it with the last element

00:00:12.085 --> 00:00:14.782
now the largest is in its final place.

00:00:14.782 --> 00:00:17.203
What is left is a smaller problem

00:00:17.203 --> 00:00:19.762
sort the first n − 1 elements

00:00:19.762 --> 00:00:20.730
the same way.

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:26.477
Trace it on 4, 2, 3, 6, 9,

00:00:26.477 --> 00:00:27.252
5.

00:00:27.252 --> 00:00:31.773
The scan starts with maxNum 4

00:00:31.773 --> 00:00:33.710
at index 0.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:35.754
2: not larger.

00:00:35.754 --> 00:00:37.765
3: not larger.

00:00:37.765 --> 00:00:41.046
6 is larger: maxNum is 6

00:00:41.046 --> 00:00:42.740
at index 3.

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:46.864
9 is larger again: index 4.

00:00:46.864 --> 00:00:48.390
5 is not.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:49.386
Then swap

00:00:49.386 --> 00:00:52.447
arr[4] gets the last element, 5

00:00:52.447 --> 00:00:54.388
and 9 goes to the end.

00:00:54.388 --> 00:00:57.000
Now the call sorts the first 5.

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:00.313
Size 5: the largest is 6

00:01:00.313 --> 00:01:02.409
which swaps with 5.

00:01:02.409 --> 00:01:05.142
Size 4: the largest, 5

00:01:05.142 --> 00:01:09.060
is already last, so it copies onto itself.

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:12.792
Size 3: 4 swaps with 3.

00:01:12.792 --> 00:01:16.070
Size 2: 3 swaps with 2.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:19.150
Size 1 is the base case

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:23.830
and the array is sorted: 2, 3, 4

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:25.680
5, 6, 9.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:26.916
In Java

00:01:26.916 --> 00:01:30.034
selectionSort takes the array and n

00:01:30.034 --> 00:01:32.660
the number of elements to sort.

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:35.860
0 or 1 element is already sorted.

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:38.521
The loop finds the largest of

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:40.512
arr[0] to arr[n − 1]

00:01:40.512 --> 00:01:42.743
and counts the comparisons.

00:01:42.743 --> 00:01:45.930
The two assignments move it to the end.

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:48.060
And the last line sorts

00:01:48.137 --> 00:01:50.846
the first n − 1, recursively.

00:01:50.846 --> 00:01:53.400
That call is the only recursion.

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:56.163
On our 6 numbers it makes

00:01:56.243 --> 00:01:57.860
15 comparisons.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:02.139
For 10, 100 and 1000 elements

00:02:02.139 --> 00:02:05.977
the count is exactly n * (n − 1)

00:02:05.977 --> 00:02:08.810
/ 2, whatever the values.

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:12.109
The scan for size k always makes

00:02:12.201 --> 00:02:15.980
k − 1 comparisons, and (n − 1)

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:19.759
+ (n − 2), and so on down to 1

00:02:19.759 --> 00:02:22.800
is n * (n - 1) / 2.

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:24.642
Now the cost.

00:02:24.642 --> 00:02:26.583
The base-case test

00:02:26.583 --> 00:02:30.757
the assignments and the swap are constant

00:02:30.757 --> 00:02:34.349
and the loop runs n - 1 times

00:02:34.349 --> 00:02:38.037
everything except the call is O(n).

00:02:38.037 --> 00:02:41.629
The call sorts n - 1 elements

00:02:41.629 --> 00:02:45.900
so by definition it costs T(n - 1).

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:49.962
O(n) means at most cn

00:02:49.962 --> 00:02:54.632
so T(n) is at most T(n - 1)

00:02:54.632 --> 00:02:55.829
plus cn.

00:02:55.829 --> 00:02:58.942
Unroll: T(n - 2)

00:02:58.942 --> 00:03:03.253
plus c * (n - 1), plus cn.

00:03:03.253 --> 00:03:04.570
And again.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:09.010
Down to T(1), plus c * 2

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:10.996
up to cn.

00:03:10.996 --> 00:03:16.137
That is at most c * n * (n + 1)

00:03:16.137 --> 00:03:18.240
over 2, plus b.

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:21.573
So T(n) is O(n²).

00:03:21.573 --> 00:03:25.622
The problem shrinks by one each time

00:03:25.622 --> 00:03:29.458
so there are n levels of recursion

00:03:29.458 --> 00:03:30.630
not log n.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:33.689
A common mistake is to write

00:03:33.785 --> 00:03:36.687
T(n - 1), plus O(n)

00:03:36.687 --> 00:03:38.138
equals O(n).

00:03:38.138 --> 00:03:40.557
Each level costs O(n)

00:03:40.557 --> 00:03:42.878
but there are n levels.

00:03:42.878 --> 00:03:45.780
Unroll before you drop the T.

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:47.461
For practice

00:03:47.546 --> 00:03:50.425
maxInArray1 finds the largest

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:52.543
of the first n elements.

00:03:52.543 --> 00:03:55.253
One element is its own maximum.

00:03:55.337 --> 00:03:57.878
Otherwise it finds the largest

00:03:57.963 --> 00:04:00.080
of the first n - 1

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:03.630
and returns the larger of that

00:04:03.737 --> 00:04:05.977
and the last element.

00:04:06.083 --> 00:04:09.390
The call costs T(n - 1)

00:04:09.497 --> 00:04:12.163
and the rest is constant

00:04:12.163 --> 00:04:16.323
so T(n) is at most T(n - 1)

00:04:16.323 --> 00:04:17.177
plus c.

00:04:17.177 --> 00:04:21.870
Unrolling gives at most cn + a: O(n).

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:24.846
The same shape as selection sort

00:04:24.846 --> 00:04:27.074
but constant work per call

00:04:27.074 --> 00:04:29.620
so linear instead of quadratic.

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:32.957
The second method, maxInArray2

00:04:32.957 --> 00:04:34.565
does it differently.

00:04:34.565 --> 00:04:37.246
It splits the range in the middle

00:04:37.246 --> 00:04:40.616
finds the maximum of each half recursively

00:04:40.616 --> 00:04:42.760
and returns the larger one.

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:45.014
This is divide and conquer

00:04:45.084 --> 00:04:47.270
divide the problem into smaller

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:49.173
problems of the same kind

00:04:49.173 --> 00:04:51.006
conquer them recursively

00:04:51.006 --> 00:04:52.910
and combine their answers.

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:56.036
maxInArray2 makes two recursive

00:04:56.114 --> 00:04:57.579
calls in each call.

00:04:57.579 --> 00:05:00.895
On 8 elements, does it make more calls

00:05:00.972 --> 00:05:02.669
than maxInArray1?

00:05:02.669 --> 00:05:03.980
Pause and think.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:10.881
Here is its call tree on 8 numbers.

00:05:10.881 --> 00:05:13.946
The first call is on 0 to 7.

00:05:13.946 --> 00:05:16.927
It calls itself on the two halves

00:05:16.927 --> 00:05:18.800
and they split again

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:22.321
down to 8 single elements: the leaves

00:05:22.321 --> 00:05:24.510
which make no further calls.

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:28.327
maxInArray1 makes 8 calls

00:05:28.327 --> 00:05:30.011
one per element.

00:05:30.011 --> 00:05:33.180
maxInArray2 makes 15.

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:35.630
In general 2n - 1

00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:38.353
one call for each of the n leaves

00:05:38.431 --> 00:05:40.220
and n - 1 for the

00:05:40.298 --> 00:05:42.009
ranges that are split.

00:05:42.009 --> 00:05:45.043
About twice as many, but still O(n)

00:05:45.121 --> 00:05:47.065
and the recursion is only

00:05:47.143 --> 00:05:49.010
about log₂ n deep.

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:52.892
Every line of maxInArray2 except

00:05:52.982 --> 00:05:55.302
the two calls is constant

00:05:55.302 --> 00:05:58.515
and each call is on half the range

00:05:58.515 --> 00:06:01.996
T(n) is at most 2T(n/2)

00:06:01.996 --> 00:06:02.710
plus c.

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:07.166
Unroll it for n = 2ˣ.

00:06:07.166 --> 00:06:09.867
Replace T(n/2)

00:06:09.867 --> 00:06:14.513
4T(n/4) + c + 2c.

00:06:14.513 --> 00:06:18.835
Again: 8T(n/8) + c

00:06:18.835 --> 00:06:20.780
2c and 4c.

00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:25.975
After x steps: 2ˣ * T(1)

00:06:25.975 --> 00:06:30.144
plus c * (1 + 2 + 4)

00:06:30.144 --> 00:06:33.524
up to 2ˣ⁻¹.

00:06:33.524 --> 00:06:37.130
That is bn + cn - c.

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:40.873
Be careful that the factor 2 multiplies

00:06:40.956 --> 00:06:43.439
everything inside the bracket.

00:06:43.439 --> 00:06:47.080
T(n) is O(n), as the call counter said.

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:50.540
Unrolling a recurrence with several

00:06:50.622 --> 00:06:52.844
calls is easy to get wrong.

00:06:52.926 --> 00:06:55.394
A recursion tree organises the

00:06:55.477 --> 00:06:58.850
same sum as a picture: one node per call

00:06:58.850 --> 00:07:01.895
with the work that call does itself.

00:07:01.895 --> 00:07:04.857
Take 2T(n/2) + cn.

00:07:04.857 --> 00:07:06.420
The root does cn.

00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:10.710
Its two children do cn/2 each

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:12.162
cn in total.

00:07:12.162 --> 00:07:16.310
Four grandchildren, cn/4 each

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:17.450
cn again.

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:23.440
At the bottom are n leaves, b each: bn.

00:07:23.790 --> 00:07:28.154
So every level above the leaves costs cn

00:07:28.154 --> 00:07:32.310
and there are log₂ n such levels.

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:35.945
T(n) is cn log₂ n, plus bn

00:07:35.945 --> 00:07:37.297
O(n log n).

00:07:37.297 --> 00:07:40.775
We will meet this recurrence again

00:07:40.775 --> 00:07:42.900
for maximum subarray.

00:07:43.480 --> 00:07:46.399
For 2T(n/2) + c

00:07:46.399 --> 00:07:50.503
the levels cost c, 2c, 4c: they grow

00:07:50.503 --> 00:07:53.787
and the bottom level, with n nodes

00:07:53.787 --> 00:07:55.520
dominates: O(n).

00:07:55.870 --> 00:07:57.310
For binary search

00:07:57.390 --> 00:07:59.710
the tree is a single chain of

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:02.750
log₂ n + 1 nodes: O(log n).

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:05.550
How the level sums change from top

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:07.950
to bottom is the whole story.

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:11.784
Unrolling and trees find an answer.

00:08:11.784 --> 00:08:15.131
The substitution method proves one:

00:08:15.131 --> 00:08:19.221
guess the form, then prove it by induction.

00:08:19.221 --> 00:08:23.312
The tree suggests that 2T(n/2)

00:08:23.312 --> 00:08:26.659
+ n, with T(1) = 1

00:08:26.659 --> 00:08:28.890
is n log₂ n + n.

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:35.000
Base case: 1 * 0 + 1 = 1.

00:08:35.350 --> 00:08:38.591
Step: assume the formula

00:08:38.684 --> 00:08:40.721
for n/2 and substitute it.

00:08:40.721 --> 00:08:44.888
2 * (the formula at n/2) + n

00:08:44.888 --> 00:08:48.870
simplifies to exactly n log₂ n + n.

00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:50.309
Be careful.

00:08:50.407 --> 00:08:54.464
A wrong proof that this is O(n) assumes

00:08:54.563 --> 00:08:58.521
T(n/2) is at most cn/2

00:08:58.521 --> 00:09:02.677
and gets cn + n: (c + 1) * n.

00:09:02.677 --> 00:09:06.734
The constant grew, so nothing is proved.

00:09:06.833 --> 00:09:09.999
The induction must prove exactly

00:09:10.098 --> 00:09:11.780
the guessed form.

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:14.890
We can also compute these recurrences

00:09:14.959 --> 00:09:16.531
exactly with a program

00:09:16.531 --> 00:09:18.309
all constants set to 1

00:09:18.309 --> 00:09:21.044
and compare them with the closed forms.

00:09:21.113 --> 00:09:23.233
Each recurrence has its formula

00:09:23.301 --> 00:09:24.600
in the next column.

00:09:24.950 --> 00:09:26.742
At 1024

00:09:26.742 --> 00:09:27.958
11 and 11,

00:09:28.022 --> 00:09:30.134
524800

00:09:30.198 --> 00:09:31.606
in both,

00:09:31.606 --> 00:09:33.782
2047 in both,

00:09:33.846 --> 00:09:35.830
and 11264

00:09:35.894 --> 00:09:37.110
in both.

00:09:37.460 --> 00:09:39.584
Every pair of columns agrees

00:09:39.584 --> 00:09:42.220
so the four closed forms are right.

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:43.729
To sum up.

00:09:43.729 --> 00:09:47.261
One: when the problem shrinks by one

00:09:47.261 --> 00:09:49.119
there are n levels

00:09:49.119 --> 00:09:52.279
selection sort is O(n²)

00:09:52.279 --> 00:09:54.881
maxInArray1 is O(n).

00:09:54.881 --> 00:09:56.832
Two: when it halves

00:09:56.925 --> 00:09:59.249
a recursion tree adds the

00:09:59.342 --> 00:10:01.200
work level by level.

00:10:01.550 --> 00:10:04.225
Three: the substitution method proves

00:10:04.297 --> 00:10:05.815
a guess by induction

00:10:05.815 --> 00:10:07.550
with the same constant.

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:11.441
Try this: draw the recursion tree of

00:10:11.539 --> 00:10:14.392
4T(n/2) + n.

00:10:14.392 --> 00:10:17.244
What is the work on level t

00:10:17.244 --> 00:10:19.900
and which level dominates?

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:22.328
Next: the master theorem

00:10:22.328 --> 00:10:25.570
which reads the answer off a, b and d.

